Section 8 of 14 — Strategic blind spots
# 5. Strategic Blind Spots

Assessed against the six dimensions specified in the mission. Direction of error
is this reviewer's **informed interpretation**; several judgements are
conditional on the unknown resource envelope.

## 5.1 Technical capability — OVERESTIMATED (confidence: Medium)
CrawDaddy and selarix are cited as differentiating assets with no assessment of
maturity, security posture, documentation, or deployability. 001A Rec 3 proposes
using CrawDaddy to win paying clients within 60 days; nothing establishes it is
production-ready or that its output is defensible to a paying client.
Simultaneously, claimed competence spans AI governance, cybersecurity, ML
security, digital assets, post-quantum cryptography, knowledge infrastructure,
blockchain forensics, and MLOps. **No asset audit exists in either document, and
this should precede any capability claim in a client-facing offer.**

## 5.2 Market readiness — OVERESTIMATED (confidence: Medium-High)
The reports establish *concern* and infer *budget*. Per A1, these are different
things. The specific gap: neither document identifies which executive owns an AI
governance budget line at a target buyer. In most organizations today, no one
does — which is precisely why AI governance deals stall at enthusiasm. Readiness
is real only in the deadline-driven segments of M3, and those are exactly the
segments the reports leave undeveloped.

## 5.3 Competition — MISIDENTIFIED rather than mis-sized (confidence: High)
Both reports name large consultancies. The real competition for QSL's realistic
first engagements is (a) the very large population of boutique and solo
consultants and fractional CISOs, and (b) the incumbent trusted advisor who
already holds the relationship. 001A's differentiation logic — "smaller size
allows agility" and "can underbid" — is the standard small-firm claim, is not
evidenced, and addresses price when the binding constraint is access. **Also
under-weighted:** the deliverables QSL would sell (policy frameworks, maturity
assessments, gap analyses) are exactly the artifacts most exposed to
commoditization by generative AI, including by QSL's own tools. Neither report
considers that its market may be structurally deflationary.

## 5.4 Capital requirements — UNDERESTIMATED, and largely unexamined (confidence: High)
Neither document states what executing its own plan costs. Unpriced items
include: proposal-writing hours, professional liability insurance, legal review
of contract templates, certifications and training, conference attendance and
travel, tooling and API spend, and — largest of all — the unbillable hours
consumed by relationship-building and grant pursuit. **001A's scoring table
assigns "Capital: 1" to several opportunities, implying near-zero capital
requirement. This is an accounting artifact: the capital required is founder
time, which the table does not price.**

## 5.5 Regulatory timing — MIXED, with an unhedged direction (confidence: Medium)
*Overestimated:* the speed at which frameworks and guidance become procurement.
*Underestimated:* the ambiguity of the current moment, and the genuine
possibility of deregulatory movement or federal preemption of state AI law
(A12). The reports treat regulatory tightening as certain and unidirectional.
A strategy dependent on a single-direction demand driver, with no hedge, is
fragile in a way neither document acknowledges.

## 5.6 Customer demand — UNKNOWN, and presented as known (confidence: High that this is the central gap)
**This is the most important finding in this review.** Across two reports and
roughly four hundred lines of analysis, there is no evidence that any specific
prospective customer has expressed interest in any specific QSL offering at any
specific price. There is no indication whether QSL has ever been paid by anyone.
The demand case is entirely top-down.

This is not a small omission; it inverts the correct order of work. The right
first activity is not packaging, pricing, website updates, or grant proposals.
It is a structured sequence of conversations with real prospective buyers, whose
purpose is to *disconfirm* the thesis. Everything in 001A's 90-day plan
presupposes an answer that has not been sought.
